WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.220
A sensory neuron responds to capsaicin, the burning chemical in chilli peppers.

2
00:00:06.580 --> 00:00:07.840
Which gene makes it react?

3
00:00:09.060 --> 00:00:12.640
That cell expresses many genes at once, none carrying labels.

4
00:00:13.960 --> 00:00:16.960
You cannot spot the sensor by looking at DNA alone.

5
00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:20.560
Instead, you find the gene by what it does.

6
00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:28.340
First, make DNA copies of the tissue's expressed genes, called cDNA.

7
00:00:29.840 --> 00:00:31.060
Divide them into pools.

8
00:00:32.560 --> 00:00:39.040
Next, pick foreign cells, like HEK293, that do not normally respond to capsaicin.

9
00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:43.760
Putting a gene into foreign cells is heterologous expression.

10
00:00:44.900 --> 00:00:48.020
Now put one pool into each dish of silent foreign cells.

11
00:00:49.360 --> 00:00:52.140
Give the cells time to make the encoded proteins.

12
00:00:53.480 --> 00:00:57.340
Then add capsaicin and watch for calcium flowing into the cells.

13
00:00:58.600 --> 00:01:01.060
Most dishes stay dark, lacking the sensor.

14
00:01:02.360 --> 00:01:04.440
One dish glows, marking the winning pool.

15
00:01:05.550 --> 00:01:08.610
That glowing dish still holds a whole pool of genes.

16
00:01:09.950 --> 00:01:13.910
Split the winning pool into smaller pools and test fresh silent cells.

17
00:01:15.210 --> 00:01:17.610
Repeat this narrowing until one clone remains.

18
00:01:18.850 --> 00:01:24.210
That is expression cloning: split pools of expressed genes until one alone creates

19
00:01:24.210 --> 00:01:25.210
the response.

20
00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:31.320
Suppose your single cloned gene makes a foreign cell respond to capsaicin and

21
00:01:31.320 --> 00:01:35.180
noxious heat. Pause and think before we go on.

22
00:01:36.400 --> 00:01:40.500
Does that prove an intact animal actually needs this gene to sense heat?

23
00:01:46.033 --> 00:01:52.233
No. Cloning shows a gene is sufficient: it gives a foreign cell the response.

24
00:01:53.533 --> 00:01:56.953
It does not prove the animal needs it, or that it works alone.

25
00:01:58.373 --> 00:02:00.413
Testing need means removing the gene.

26
00:02:01.733 --> 00:02:06.413
In 2018, Vandewauw and colleagues showed three heat channels act in parallel.

27
00:02:07.393 --> 00:02:12.693
Single removals kept acute heat withdrawal; only losing all three abolished it.

28
00:02:13.667 --> 00:02:18.807
Controls for expression itself check that the procedure alone did not cause the

29
00:02:18.807 --> 00:02:24.327
effect. Removing a gene from the start also lets development compensate.

30
00:02:25.627 --> 00:02:29.607
And making a cell pass current never proves the protein lines the pore.

31
00:02:31.067 --> 00:02:34.847
Necessity, sufficiency and pore identity are three separate claims.

32
00:02:35.967 --> 00:02:39.847
With those checks, expression cloning unlocked landmark proteins.

33
00:02:41.307 --> 00:02:47.867
In 1992, Smith and Harland injected RNA pools into ultraviolet-treated embryos

34
00:02:48.427 --> 00:02:49.807
lacking dorsal structures.

35
00:02:51.267 --> 00:02:53.967
Narrowing the pools found Noggin, which rescued them.

36
00:02:55.147 --> 00:03:01.587
In 1997, He and Tessier-Lavigne screened cDNA pools for cells binding a tagged cue.

37
00:03:02.347 --> 00:03:07.027
They found neuropilin, the receiver of Sema3A, a repulsive cue.

38
00:03:08.083 --> 00:03:14.823
Also in 1997, Caterina and colleagues in David Julius's laboratory answered our

39
00:03:14.823 --> 00:03:21.523
opening question. They screened sensory-neuron cDNA pools for capsaicin-evoked

40
00:03:21.523 --> 00:03:29.383
calcium influx. The clone they recovered, TRPV1, encoded a channel also activated by

41
00:03:29.383 --> 00:03:35.363
noxious heating. Whenever a response hides its gene, narrow expressed pools until

42
00:03:35.363 --> 00:03:36.463
one reproduces it.
